## Known UI Decisions and Rationale

### Why Send Inquiry is absent not disabled
A disabled button invites the user to wonder why. An absent button communicates that the option does not exist for this property. Properties with B_score = 0 are fundamentally incompatible — there is nothing to inquire about.

### Why session filters never write to Firestore
Session filters are a temporary exploration tool. Writing them to Firestore would alter the user's bilateral filter profile and change their match results permanently. The distinction between hard constraints (permanent, in Firestore) and session overrides (temporary, local state only) is architecturally critical.

### Why the Home screen shows "Compatible properties" not "Nearby homes"
RentEase does not sort by proximity — it sorts by TOPSIS Ci score. Using "Nearby homes" implies geographic sorting which is incorrect. "Compatible properties" or "Best matches" correctly communicates the system's value proposition.

### Why match display should be consistent
The Ci score (0.00–1.00) is the precise TOPSIS output. On the home screen carousel a percentage format (86% match) is more user-friendly. On the search screen the raw Ci score (Ci 0.86) provides technical precision for comparison. Whatever format is chosen, it must be consistent within each screen context.

### Why distance is computed via latlong2 not Maps API
The Google Maps Distance Matrix API charges per request. With potentially hundreds of property-tenant distance computations running at profile-save time, this would be cost-prohibitive. The `latlong2` package produces a straight-line distance from stored coordinates — free, fast, and sufficient for the filtering and ranking purposes of this system.

